WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 179: Dwight D. Eisenhower: Supreme Allied Commander and D-Day (1942–1945)

1
00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:05.600
[Rebecca] Welcome to Most Fascinating Stories.

2
00:00:05.600 --> 00:00:06.800
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

3
00:00:06.800 --> 00:00:09.920
[Rebecca] Today, we begin our journey into the life of Dwight D.

4
00:00:09.920 --> 00:00:10.960
[Rebecca] Eisenhower.

5
00:00:10.960 --> 00:00:23.680
[Rebecca] Before he presided over the peace and prosperity of the 1950s as America's 34th president, Eisenhower carried the weight of the free world on his shoulders as supreme allied commander during the Second World War.

6
00:00:23.680 --> 00:00:31.840
[Rebecca] Here is George with the story of the Kansas farm boy who commanded the liberation of Europe.

7
00:00:31.840 --> 00:00:45.760
[George] Dwight David Eisenhower was born in Denison, Texas, in October 1890, but grew up in the rugged, dusty frontier town of Abilene, Kansas, once the wild cattle railhead of Wild Bill Hickok.

8
00:00:45.760 --> 00:00:57.200
[George] He was the third of seven sons born to David and Ida Eisenhower, devout members of the Pacifist River Brethren sect who lived in near destitution after a failed general store business.

9
00:00:57.200 --> 00:01:10.320
[George] The family grew their own vegetables, raised chickens, and Ida read daily from the family Bible, instilling in young Ike an abiding horror of arrogance, wasteful cruelty, and religious intolerance.

10
00:01:10.320 --> 00:01:17.1000
[George] Athletic, quick-tempered, and possessed of a radiant, infectious smile, Ike excelled at football and baseball.

11
00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:27.200
[George] Seeking a free college education to relieve his impoverished parents of financial strain, he applied to the United States Military Academy at West Point.

12
00:01:27.200 --> 00:01:34.960
[George] Graduating in 1915 as part of the celebrated class the stars fell on, 59 of whom would become generals.

13
00:01:34.960 --> 00:01:42.960
[George] Eisenhower was bitterly disappointed during World War I when he was assigned to command Tank Corps training at Camp Colt, Pennsylvania.

14
00:01:42.960 --> 00:01:48.480
[George] Missing overseas combat in France entirely while his classmates won battle glory.

15
00:01:48.480 --> 00:01:59.920
[George] The austere, Bible-reading atmosphere of the Kansas Prairie instilled in Eisenhower a deep personal modesty and an absolute refusal to engage in vainglorious self-promotion throughout his military career.

16
00:02:01.000 --> 00:02:13.320
[George] West Point provided the rigorous technical foundation, but it was Eisenhower's Kansas character, humble, direct, and instinctively democratic, that won him the trust of ordinary foot soldiers.

17
00:02:13.320 --> 00:02:22.680
[George] During the lean interwar decades, Eisenhower languished for sixteen long years as a mere major in a peacetime army starved of funds and public interest.

18
00:02:22.680 --> 00:02:26.760
[George] Yet these years served as the crucible of his strategic genius.

19
00:02:26.760 --> 00:02:41.000
[George] In 1919, he accompanied the first transcontinental military motor convoy across America, a grueling 62-day trek over unpaved dirt roads that planted the seed for his later creation of the interstate highway system.

20
00:02:41.000 --> 00:02:57.720
[George] In Panama, he served under Brigadier General Fox Connor, a brilliant military intellectual who took Eisenhower as his protege, forcing him to read Carl von Clauswitz and predicting that another catastrophic world war was inevitable and that an officer named George C.

21
00:02:57.960 --> 00:03:08.520
[George] Marshall would lead it, graduating first in his class of 245 senior officers from the prestigious command and general staff college at Fort Leavenworth.

22
00:03:08.520 --> 00:03:16.840
[George] Eisenhower spent seven grueling years in Washington and the Philippines as the indispensable chief of staff to General Douglas MacArthur.

23
00:03:16.840 --> 00:03:27.160
[George] The volatile, theatrical MacArthur taught Eisenhower how to manage massive military bureaucracies, and showed him exactly what kind of arrogant general he never wanted to be.

24
00:03:27.160 --> 00:03:40.280
[George] His grueling interwar apprenticeship under visionary military mentors taught Eisenhower that modern global warfare demanded masterly strategic organization, logistics, and total command of staff operations.

25
00:03:40.280 --> 00:03:51.680
[George] During those long peacetime years in Panama and the Philippines, Eisenhower quietly studied the operational requirements of mechanized warfare, preparing for the conflict he knew was coming.

26
00:03:51.680 --> 00:03:56.320
[George] Five days after Pearl Harbor, Army Chief of Staff General George C.

27
00:03:56.320 --> 00:04:02.160
[George] Marshall summoned 51-year-old Brigadier General Eisenhower to the war department in Washington.

28
00:04:02.160 --> 00:04:07.760
[George] Marshall laid out the catastrophic military situation in the Pacific and asked bluntly.

29
00:04:07.760 --> 00:04:10.240
[George] What should be our general line of action?

30
00:04:10.240 --> 00:04:24.800
[George] Eisenhower requested a few hours, returned to his typewriter, and drafted an incisive, five-page strategic plan prioritizing the defense of Australia and the Pacific Sea lanes while insisting upon a Germany-first grand strategy.

31
00:04:24.800 --> 00:04:31.840
[George] Marshall was dazzled by Eisenhower's clarity, moral courage, and refusal to play bureaucratic politics.

32
00:04:31.840 --> 00:04:41.600
[George] In June 1942, Marshall vaulted Eisenhower over 366 senior officers to appoint him commander of the European Theater of Operations.

33
00:04:41.600 --> 00:04:50.640
[George] In November 1942, Eisenhower commanded Operation Torch, the massive Allied amphibious invasion of French North Africa.

34
00:04:50.640 --> 00:05:00.400
[George] Though American troops suffered a bloody, humiliating baptism of fire against German panzers at Kasserine Pass, Eisenhower demonstrated his supreme gift.

35
00:05:00.400 --> 00:05:09.280
[George] Learning instantly from tactical defeat, sacking ineffective commanders like Lloyd Fredendah, and replacing them with fighting generals like George S.

36
00:05:09.280 --> 00:05:11.360
[George] Patton and Omar Bradley.

37
00:05:11.360 --> 00:05:22.800
[George] Marshall recognized in Eisenhower the rare combination of brilliant operational clarity, diplomatic tact, and absolute personal selflessness necessary to lead an allied coalition.

38
00:05:22.800 --> 00:05:33.160
[George] Marshall's unwavering confidence in Eisenhower was vindicated at every stage of the North African campaign as Icke adapted rapidly to the harsh realities of battlefield command.

39
00:05:33.480 --> 00:05:41.480
[George] Eisenhower's greatest military achievement was not tactical maneuver, but the creation of an entirely unified Allied command.

40
00:05:41.480 --> 00:05:50.520
[George] In previous coalitions throughout history, national jealousies, clashing egos, and separate chains of command had crippled military operations.

41
00:05:50.520 --> 00:05:53.800
[George] Eisenhower refused to permit national rivalry.

42
00:05:53.800 --> 00:06:05.240
[George] He declared that any officer who insulted an ally would be fired on the spot, famously sending home an American colonel who called a British colleague, a British son of a bitch, explaining.

43
00:06:05.240 --> 00:06:11.880
[George] I don't mind that you called him a son of a bitch, but I will not tolerate that you called him a British son of a bitch.

44
00:06:11.880 --> 00:06:20.840
[George] Operating from his headquarters in London and a modest trailer in the English countryside, Eisenhower managed the volatile temperaments of Winston Churchill.

45
00:06:20.840 --> 00:06:30.600
[George] The arrogant and prickly general Charles de Gaulle, the flamboyant British Field Marshal Bernard Montgomery, and the rebellious American tank commander George S.

46
00:06:30.600 --> 00:06:31.400
[George] Patton.

47
00:06:31.400 --> 00:06:41.000
[George] By treating all with unfailing patience, dignity, and absolute fairness, Ike became the indispensable glue that held the Allied crusade together.

48
00:06:41.000 --> 00:06:54.040
[George] Eisenhower's iron rule against national chauvinism transformed a fractious coalition of clashing egos and proud nations into an indivisible, brotherly fighting force capable of liberating an entire continent.

49
00:06:54.040 --> 00:07:04.440
[George] He possessed a unique psychological genius for soothing the volcanic tempers of prima donna subordinates like Montgomery and Patton, keeping everyone focused on the common enemy.

50
00:07:04.440 --> 00:07:16.400
[George] In early June 1944, nearly three million Allied soldiers, sailors, and airmen stood poised in southern England for Operation Overlord, the cross-channel invasion of Normandy.

51
00:07:16.640 --> 00:07:29.520
[George] But on June 4, the worst summer gale in forty years battered the English Channel, producing ten-foot swells, driving rain, and low cloud cover that made air support and naval bombardment impossible.

52
00:07:29.520 --> 00:07:33.920
[George] Eisenhower was forced to order a harrowing 24-hour postponement.

53
00:07:33.920 --> 00:07:43.840
[George] At 4 o'clock on the morning of Monday, June 5, 1944, Eisenhower met with his top commanders in the library of Southwick House near Portsmouth.

54
00:07:43.840 --> 00:07:48.320
[George] Outside, rain lashed the windows and wind howled through the trees.

55
00:07:48.320 --> 00:07:55.120
[George] Group Captain James Stagg, his chief meteorological officer, delivered an astonishing forecast.

56
00:07:55.120 --> 00:08:00.000
[George] A temporary 36-hour break in the storm would begin the following morning.

57
00:08:00.000 --> 00:08:10.640
[George] If Eisenhower called off the invasion now, the massive tidal windows would close, forcing a two-week postponement that would destroy surprise and crush Allied morale.

58
00:08:10.640 --> 00:08:23.520
[George] For five agonizing minutes, Eisenhower sat in absolute silence, staring into the carpet, carrying the lives of 150,000 men and the future of Western civilization upon his shoulders.

59
00:08:23.520 --> 00:08:27.360
[George] Slowly, he looked up, his face calm and resolute.

60
00:08:27.360 --> 00:08:28.880
[George] Okay, we'll go.

61
00:08:28.880 --> 00:08:32.640
[George] The greatest armada in the history of the world was set in motion.

62
00:08:32.640 --> 00:08:43.280
[George] The fateful decision to launch the invasion into the brief weather window revealed the ultimate courage of Supreme Command, the willingness to shoulder total responsibility alone.

63
00:08:43.280 --> 00:08:54.640
[George] In the silence of Southwick House, Eisenhower's solitary decision exemplified the terrible lonely burden of high command, where the lives of thousands hang upon a single spoken word.

64
00:08:54.640 --> 00:09:03.400
[George] Before retiring on the eve of D-Day, Eisenhower visited the young paratroopers of the 101st Airborne Division at Green M Common Airfield.

65
00:09:03.400 --> 00:09:17.400
[George] Walking among the men with their faces blackened with burnt cork, Eisenhower joked with them, asked about their families in Kansas and Michigan, and watched with tears in his eyes as C 47 transports took off into the night sky.

66
00:09:17.400 --> 00:09:25.800
[George] In his wallet, Eisenhower carried a small, penciled press release to be issued only if the invasion ended in catastrophic disaster.

67
00:09:25.800 --> 00:09:32.440
[George] Our landings in the Cherberghaver area have failed to gain a satisfactory foothold, and I have withdrawn the troops.

68
00:09:32.440 --> 00:09:37.800
[George] My decision to attack at this time and place was based upon the best information available.

69
00:09:37.800 --> 00:09:42.040
[George] If any blame or fault attaches to the attempt, it is mine alone.

70
00:09:42.040 --> 00:09:54.200
[George] On the morning of June 6, 1944, Allied soldiers braved devastating machine-gun fire and artillery on the sands of Omaha, Utah, Gold, Juneau, and Sword Beaches.

71
00:09:54.200 --> 00:10:03.400
[George] By nightfall, despite horrific casualties, the beachhead was secured, the Atlantic Wall was breached, and Hitler's empire was doomed.

72
00:10:03.400 --> 00:10:14.520
[George] That solitary penciled note accepting full personal blame for any potential failure remains one of the most noble documents of moral leadership in the entire history of military warfare.

73
00:10:14.520 --> 00:10:24.440
[George] The paratroopers who looked into Ike's smiling eyes that fateful knight saw not a distant commander, but a loving father who shared their danger and believed in their victory.

74
00:10:24.440 --> 00:10:37.000
[George] Over the next eleven months, Eisenhower directed the Allied advance across France, through the liberation of Paris, and repelled Hitler's desperate winter counteroffensive in the Ardennes during the Battle of the Bulge.

75
00:10:37.000 --> 00:10:54.400
[George] In April 1945, entering the liberated Oadruff concentration camp, Eisenhower was so shaken by the piles of emaciated corpses and torture chambers that he forced every available American soldier and local German citizen to tour the camp, famously declaring.

76
00:11:02.480 --> 00:11:15.200
[George] On May 7, 1945, at Eisenhower's forward headquarters in a red brick schoolhouse in Rheims, France, General Alfred Jaudel signed the unconditional surrender of the German armed forces.

77
00:11:15.200 --> 00:11:20.640
[George] Eisenhower cabled a simple, immortal message to the combined chiefs of staff.

78
00:11:20.640 --> 00:11:28.160
[George] The mission of this Allied force was fulfilled at 2 41, local time, May 7, 1945.

79
00:11:28.160 --> 00:11:37.840
[George] The barefoot farm boy from Abilene had led the free world to victory over Nazi barbarism, becoming the most revered and beloved figure on the face of the earth.

80
00:11:37.840 --> 00:11:50.000
[George] Dwight Eisenhower's triumphant crusade across Europe cemented his status as a modern Cincinnatus, a military conqueror who loved peace far more than war and fought only to secure human liberty.

81
00:11:50.000 --> 00:12:04.000
[George] Upon his triumphant return to America, Eisenhower spoke at London's Guildhall and before Congress, expressing his enduring conviction that the humble soldier who died in combat was the true hero of the war.

82
00:12:04.000 --> 00:12:04.800
[Rebecca] Dwight D.

83
00:12:04.800 --> 00:12:13.360
[Rebecca] Eisenhower liberated Europe with a combination of military strategic mastery, diplomatic grace, and unshakable humble character.

84
00:12:13.360 --> 00:12:16.480
[Rebecca] But his service to the nation was far from over.

85
00:12:16.480 --> 00:12:22.080
[Rebecca] In 1952, both major political parties begged him to run for president.

86
00:12:22.080 --> 00:12:25.760
[Rebecca] Next week, we turned to Eisenhower's two term presidency.

87
00:12:25.760 --> 00:12:38.280
[Rebecca] The end of the Korean War, the creation of the interstate highway System, the Little Rock Central High Crisis, the U 2 spy plane affair, and his prophetic warning regarding the military industrial complex.

88
00:12:38.280 --> 00:12:46.040
[Rebecca] Until then, thank you for listening to most fascinating stories.

